AI Prototype Summary
Model used: Claude Fable 5.1

AI Chat Transcript


User Prompt

Write SystemVerilog HDL to time multiplex a single seven segment decoder (that decodes from four bits to a common anode seven segment display) to decode two sets of input bits and drive two sets of seven output bits.



AI Response

The file contains two modules: hex_to_7seg (the single combinational decoder, active-low for common anode) and tdm_seven_seg, which toggles a sel bit each clock, muxes din0/din1 into the one decoder, and latches the result into seg0 or seg1. Each output holds its value and refreshes every other cycle, so the displays never flicker even though only one decoder exists.
If you want a real chip-level multiplexed display (one shared segment bus plus per-digit anode enables) instead of two independent 7-bit outputs, that’s a small variation — let me know.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// hex_to_7seg
//   Combinational 4-bit -&gt; 7-segment decoder for a COMMON-ANODE display.
//   Segments are active-LOW (0 = lit).  Bit order: seg[6:0] = {g,f,e,d,c,b,a}
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
module hex_to_7seg (
    input  logic [3:0] bin,
    output logic [6:0] seg      // {g,f,e,d,c,b,a}, active low
);
    always_comb begin
        unique case (bin)      //        gfedcba
            4'h0: seg = 7'b1000000;
            4'h1: seg = 7'b1111001;
            4'h2: seg = 7'b0100100;
            4'h3: seg = 7'b0110000;
            4'h4: seg = 7'b0011001;
            4'h5: seg = 7'b0010010;
            4'h6: seg = 7'b0000010;
            4'h7: seg = 7'b1111000;
            4'h8: seg = 7'b0000000;
            4'h9: seg = 7'b0010000;
            4'hA: seg = 7'b0001000;
            4'hB: seg = 7'b0000011;
            4'hC: seg = 7'b1000110;
            4'hD: seg = 7'b0100001;
            4'hE: seg = 7'b0000110;
            4'hF: seg = 7'b0001110;
            default: seg = 7'b1111111;   // all off
        endcase
    end
endmodule


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// tdm_seven_seg
//   Time-multiplexes ONE hex_to_7seg instance between two 4-bit inputs.
//   Each clock the mux alternates inputs; the decoder result is latched
//   into the matching output register, so both 7-bit outputs are held
//   continuously and each refreshes every other clock.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
module tdm_seven_seg (
    input  logic       clk,
    input  logic       rst_n,      // async active-low reset
    input  logic [3:0] din0,
    input  logic [3:0] din1,
    output logic [6:0] seg0,       // decoded din0, active low
    output logic [6:0] seg1        // decoded din1, active low
);
    logic       sel;               // 0 -&gt; din0 this cycle, 1 -&gt; din1
    logic [3:0] bin_mux;
    logic [6:0] seg_dec;

    // Input mux feeding the single decoder
    assign bin_mux = sel ? din1 : din0;

    hex_to_7seg u_dec (
        .bin (bin_mux),
        .seg (seg_dec)
    );

    // Output demux: capture decoder result into the selected output register
    always_ff @(posedge clk or negedge rst_n) begin
        if (!rst_n) begin
            sel  &lt;= 1'b0;
            seg0 &lt;= 7'b1111111;    // blank on reset
            seg1 &lt;= 7'b1111111;
        end else begin
            if (sel) seg1 &lt;= seg_dec;
            else     seg0 &lt;= seg_dec;
            sel &lt;= ~sel;
        end
    end
endmodule

New chat with lab 1 files attached:


User Prompt

Write SystemVerilog HDL to time multiplex a single seven segment decoder (that decodes from four bits to a common anode seven segment display) to decode two sets of input bits and drive two sets of seven output bits. Use the seven segment decoder and oscillator provided in the attached files.



AI Response

How it works:
seven_segment_mux.sv — the multiplexer. Your counter module divides the clock; each time it wraps, a 1-bit digit register toggles. That bit selects which nibble (s0 or s1) feeds the single seven_segment_decoder instance, and on the output side routes the decoded pattern to seg0 or seg1 while blanking the other with 7’b1111111 (off for common anode). It also outputs active-low anode enables an0/an1, which you’ll want if the two displays physically share segment lines and are switched through transistors — ignore them if your displays are fully separate. lab_mux_top.sv — instantiates HSOSC exactly as in lab1_az.sv and the mux with MAX_COUNT = 240_000, so at 48 MHz each display refreshes at 100 Hz (well above the ~60 Hz flicker threshold). reset keeps your active-low pushbutton convention. seven_segment_mux_tb.sv — same assert/$display style as your other testbenches, with a small MAX_COUNT so it runs fast. It checks the initial phase after reset, that outputs follow input changes immediately, and sweeps all 16 values through both displays.
You’ll need to add s1[3:0], seg1[6:0], and the anode pins to pin_assignment.pdc for whatever sites you wire them to. Compile it alongside the unchanged counter.sv and seven_segment_decoder.sv.
// Purpose: Time multiplexes ONE seven_segment_decoder between two 4-bit inputs
//          (s0, s1) and drives two separate 7-bit common-anode segment outputs
//          (seg0, seg1).
//
//          A free-running counter (the provided counter module) toggles a
//          1-bit "digit" select every MAX_COUNT clock cycles. The select
//          chooses which input nibble is fed to the single decoder; the
//          decoder output is routed to the active display while the other
//          display is blanked (all 1's = off for common anode). The switching
//          is fast enough that both digits appear continuously lit.
//
//          an0 / an1 are active-LOW common-anode enables, provided in case
//          the two displays physically share segment wires and need their
//          anodes switched externally (e.g. via PNP transistors).
//
//          With a 48 MHz clock and MAX_COUNT = 240_000, each digit is
//          refreshed at 100 Hz (200 Hz toggle rate) -&gt; no visible flicker.

module seven_segment_mux #(
    parameter WIDTH     = 18,
    parameter MAX_COUNT = 240_000
)(
    input   logic       clk,
    input   logic       reset,  // active high, asynchronous
    input   logic [3:0] s0,     // value for display 0
    input   logic [3:0] s1,     // value for display 1
    output  logic [6:0] seg0,   // segments for display 0 (common anode)
    output  logic [6:0] seg1,   // segments for display 1 (common anode)
    output  logic       an0,    // active-low anode enable, display 0
    output  logic       an1     // active-low anode enable, display 1
);

    localparam logic [6:0] BLANK = 7'b1111111;  // all segments off

    logic [WIDTH-1:0]   count;
    logic               digit;  // 0 -&gt; display 0 active, 1 -&gt; display 1 active
    logic [3:0]         s_sel;
    logic [6:0]         seg;

    // Refresh-rate divider (provided counter module)
    counter #(WIDTH, MAX_COUNT) refresh_counter(clk, reset, 1'b1, count);

    // Toggle the active digit each time the counter wraps
    always_ff @(posedge clk, posedge reset) begin
        if (reset)                      digit &lt;= 1'b0;
        else if (count == MAX_COUNT-1)  digit &lt;= ~digit;
    end

    // Input multiplexer: pick which nibble goes to the single decoder
    assign s_sel = digit ? s1 : s0;

    // The single shared decoder
    seven_segment_decoder decoder(s_sel, seg);

    // Output demultiplexer: route decoded pattern to the active display,
    // blank the other one.
    assign seg0 = digit ? BLANK : seg;
    assign seg1 = digit ? seg   : BLANK;

    // Active-low anode enables
    assign an0 = digit;
    assign an1 = ~digit;

endmodule
// Purpose: Top level for the dual seven-segment display driver. Instantiates the
//          internal high-speed oscillator (48 MHz) and the time-multiplexed
//          seven-segment driver, which shares one decoder between two 4-bit
//          inputs and drives two 7-bit common-anode segment outputs.

module lab_mux_top(
    input   logic [3:0] s0,     // switch input for display 0
    input   logic [3:0] s1,     // switch input for display 1
    input   logic       reset,  // active-low pushbutton (same polarity as lab1_az)
    output  logic [6:0] seg0,   // segments for display 0
    output  logic [6:0] seg1,   // segments for display 1
    output  logic       an0,    // active-low anode enable, display 0
    output  logic       an1     // active-low anode enable, display 1
);

    logic clk;

    // Internal high-speed oscillator, 48 MHz (CLKHF_DIV default = "0b00")
    HSOSC hf_osc(.CLKHFPU(1'b1), .CLKHFEN(1'b1), .CLKHF(clk));

    // 48 MHz / 240_000 = 200 Hz digit toggle -&gt; 100 Hz refresh per display
    seven_segment_mux #(18, 240_000) display_mux(
        .clk(clk),
        .reset(~reset),
        .s0(s0),
        .s1(s1),
        .seg0(seg0),
        .seg1(seg1),
        .an0(an0),
        .an1(an1)
    );

endmodule
`timescale 1 ns/1 ns

// Testbench for seven_segment_mux. Uses a small MAX_COUNT so the digit select
// toggles every 4 clock cycles and the whole sequence simulates quickly.

module seven_segment_mux_tb();
    logic       clk;
    logic       reset;
    logic [3:0] s0, s1;
    logic [6:0] seg0, seg1;
    logic       an0, an1;

    localparam MAX_COUNT = 4;

    seven_segment_mux #(3, MAX_COUNT) dut(clk, reset, s0, s1, seg0, seg1, an0, an1);

    // Reference decoder so the expected pattern comes from the same lookup table
    logic [3:0] ref_in;
    logic [6:0] ref_seg;
    seven_segment_decoder ref_dec(ref_in, ref_seg);

    // generate a clock
    always begin
        clk = 0; #5;
        clk = 1; #5;
    end

    // Check both outputs against the expected pattern for the active digit
    task check(input logic exp_digit);
        begin
            ref_in = exp_digit ? s1 : s0;
            #1;
            if (exp_digit == 0) begin
                assert (seg0 == ref_seg && seg1 == 7'b1111111 && an0 == 0 && an1 == 1)
                    $display("PASSED! Display 0 shows %h, display 1 blanked at time: %0t.", s0, $time);
                else
                    $error("FAILED! Display 0 active: seg0=%b seg1=%b an0=%b an1=%b at time: %0t.", seg0, seg1, an0, an1, $time);
            end else begin
                assert (seg1 == ref_seg && seg0 == 7'b1111111 && an1 == 0 && an0 == 1)
                    $display("PASSED! Display 1 shows %h, display 0 blanked at time: %0t.", s1, $time);
                else
                    $error("FAILED! Display 1 active: seg0=%b seg1=%b an0=%b an1=%b at time: %0t.", seg0, seg1, an0, an1, $time);
            end
        end
    endtask

    initial begin
        reset = 1;
        s0 = 4'h3;
        s1 = 4'hA;
        #22 reset = 0;

        // After reset, display 0 is active for MAX_COUNT cycles, then display 1
        @(negedge clk); check(0);
        repeat (MAX_COUNT) @(negedge clk);
        check(1);
        repeat (MAX_COUNT) @(negedge clk);
        check(0);

        // Change inputs mid-frame: outputs must follow immediately
        s0 = 4'h7;
        s1 = 4'hE;
        @(negedge clk); check(0);
        repeat (MAX_COUNT) @(negedge clk);
        check(1);

        // Sweep all 16 values through both displays
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; 16; i++) begin
            s0 = i;
            s1 = 15 - i;
            repeat (MAX_COUNT) @(negedge clk);
            check(0);
            repeat (MAX_COUNT) @(negedge clk);
            check(1);
        end

        #100 $stop;
    end

endmodule



Summary
The two attempts differed a lot in cost. Response 1 took around 20 seconds, while response 2, with my lab 1 files attached, took around 6 minutes.
Response 1 compiles and the code makes sense, and it used one syntax I haven’t seen before: unique case. One difference is that it did not use the high speed oscillator because the instruction didn’t tell it to, adding a little bit of resistance integrating it into our existing workflow. This is a caveat I notice in a lot of AI assisted implementations, where the user didn’t include enough context resulting in an incomplete AI response.
Response 2 was much closer to what the lab instruction demands and what my code style is. Attaching my lab 1 files got the model to reuse my counter and seven_segment_decoder as well as having a template to go off of. However, again, it did not include the counter module, so it still requires some human processing before we can use the response on its own.

AI Prototype Summary
Model used: Gemini 3.6 Thinking

AI Chat Transcript


User Prompt

Write SystemVerilog HDL to leverage the internal high speed oscillator in the Lattice UP5K FPGA and blink an LED at 2 Hz. Take full advantage of SystemVerilog syntax, for example, using logic instead of wire and reg.



AI Response

// Top-level module to blink an LED at 2 Hz using the iCE40 UltraPlus internal oscillator
module led_blinker_2hz (
    output logic led = 1'b0
);

    // Internal clock signal generated by the high-speed oscillator primitive
    logic clk_48m;

    // Instantiate Lattice iCE40 UP5K High-Speed Oscillator primitive (SB_HFOSC)
    // CLKHF_DIV configuration:
    //   "0b00" -&gt; 48 MHz
    //   "0b01" -&gt; 24 MHz
    //   "0b10" -&gt; 12 MHz
    //   "0b11" -&gt;  6 MHz
    SB_HFOSC #(
        .CLKHF_DIV("0b00")  // Configure oscillator output to 48 MHz
    ) hfosc_inst (
        .CLKHFPU(1'b1),     // Power up the oscillator (1 = active)
        .CLKHFEN(1'b1),     // Enable output clock signal (1 = active)
        .CLKHF(clk_48m)     // Clock output port
    );

    // Counter frequency math:
    // To achieve a 2 Hz blink rate (period = 0.5 s), the LED toggles twice per second (every 0.25 s).
    // Toggles every N cycles: N = f_clk / (2 * f_target) = 48,000,000 / (2 * 2) = 12,000,000
    localparam int CLK_FREQ_HZ    = 48_000_000;
    localparam int TARGET_FREQ_HZ = 2;
    localparam int TOGGLE_COUNT   = (CLK_FREQ_HZ / (2 * TARGET_FREQ_HZ)) - 1;

    // 24-bit counter required to reach 11,999,999 (2^24 = 16,777,216)
    logic [23:0] counter = '0;

    // Sequential counter and LED state toggle block
    always_ff @(posedge clk_48m) begin
        if (counter == TOGGLE_COUNT) begin
            counter &lt;= '0;
            led     &lt;= ~led;
        end else begin
            counter &lt;= counter + 1'b1;
        end
    end

endmodule
Design Details & SystemVerilog Syntax Lattice Primitive (SB_HFOSC): The SB_HFOSC primitive is the hard macro for the high-speed oscillator on iCE40 UltraPlus (UP5K) FPGAs. It outputs a nominal 48 MHz clock signal when .CLKHF_DIV(“0b00”) is selected. If using Lattice Radiant IP modules, this primitive may also be wrapped under the HSOSC component name.
logic Data Type: Replaces legacy wire and reg declarations, eliminating ambiguity between net and register types across internal signals and port declarations.
always_ff procedural block: Enforces register synthesis intent for sequential clock-driven logic.
localparam int and Literal formatting: Uses integer-typed constants with literal underscores (48_000_000) for clear clock arithmetic readability.
Fill literal (’0): SystemVerilog syntax to reset all bits of a vector without explicitly sizing 24’d0.



User Prompt

Your code failed to synthesize.



AI Response

The synthesis failure on the Lattice iCE40 UltraPlus (UP5K) is almost certainly caused by one of three issues specific to Lattice synthesis engines (LSE/Synplify Pro) or your chosen toolchain flow:

Toolchain Primitive Name Mismatch (SB_HFOSC vs. HSOSC) Lattice Radiant: Uses the primitive name HSOSC. If you instantiate SB_HFOSC in Radiant, synthesis throws an unknown module error.

iCEcube2 / Yosys / Open-Source (nextpnr): Uses the primitive name SB_HFOSC.

Inline Port & Variable Initialization Declarations like output logic led = 1’b0; or logic [23:0] counter = ’0; are valid in SystemVerilog simulation, but many FPGA synthesizers (especially Lattice LSE) reject inline initializers on port declarations or variables. Initialization should be handled via an initial block (for power-up defaults) or an explicit reset signal.
Strict SystemVerilog Synthesizer Frontends Certain LSE or older Synplify versions fail on unsized literals (’0) or typed local parameters (localparam int).

Universal, Synthesis-Safe SystemVerilog Code For Open-Source (Yosys/nextpnr) or Lattice iCEcube2:
module led_blinker_2hz (
    output logic led
);

    logic clk_48m;

    // Hard primitive for iCEcube2 / Yosys open-source flow
    SB_HFOSC #(
        .CLKHF_DIV("0b00")  // 48 MHz output
    ) hfosc_inst (
        .CLKHFPU(1'b1),
        .CLKHFEN(1'b1),
        .CLKHF(clk_48m)
    );

    // 48 MHz / (2 * 2 Hz) = 12,000,000 cycles per toggle
    localparam [23:0] TOGGLE_COUNT = 24'd11_999_999;

    logic [23:0] counter;

    // Power-on state initialization for FPGA synthesis
    initial begin
        counter = 24'd0;
        led     = 1'b0;
    end

    always_ff @(posedge clk_48m) begin
        if (counter == TOGGLE_COUNT) begin
            counter &lt;= 24'd0;
            led     &lt;= ~led;
        end else begin
            counter &lt;= counter + 1'b1;
        end
    end

endmodule
For Lattice Radiant: If using Lattice Radiant, replace the primitive instantiation block above with HSOSC:
   // Hard primitive for Lattice Radiant software
    HSOSC #(
        .CLKHF_DIV("0b00")  // 48 MHz output
    ) hfosc_inst (
        .CLKHFPU(1'b1),
        .CLKHFEN(1'b1),
        .CLKHF(clk_48m)
    );
Synthesis Checklist File Extension & Tool Settings: Ensure your file is saved as .sv (not .v). If using iCEcube2 or Radiant, ensure the tool project options are set to SystemVerilog mode rather than Verilog-2001.
Library Inclusions: If using Radiant, ensure the target family is set to iCE40UP so the HSOSC macro is visible to the synthesizer.



Summary
When I asked Gemini to implement the LED blinker at first, there is one obvious mistake in its code:
module led_blinker_2hz (
    output logic led = 1'b0
);
Gemini declared an initialization value directly inside the blinker module’s output port list, which is illegal. Thus, the code quality of Gemini remains questionable, even though the rest of the code looks clean and well-documented. However, when I told Gemini that its code did not synthesize, even given this minimal information, Gemini was able to pinpoint the issue and fix its own code in one go. This proves that the model has a strong debugging ability. Therefore, in the future, I’d be curious to see what AI can do as a debugging buddy, as it seems to have quite some potential.

AI Prototype Summary
Model used: Claude Haiku 4.5

AI Chat Transcript

Prototype A


User Prompt

Target device: Lattice iCE40 UP5K FPGA with internal high-speed oscillator (~20 MHz).
Write synthesizable SystemVerilog to scan a 4x4 matrix keypad and display the last two hex keys pressed on a dual 7‑segment display. Implement:
A clock divider that derives a scan clock on the order of 100–200 Hz from the internal oscillator. A keypad scanning controller that iterates one active‑low column at a time and samples active‑low rows, registering at most one key per press (debounce‑by‑design), ignoring additional presses while any key is held, and allowing a new registration only after release. A top level that updates two hex digits (older and most recent) when a new key is registered and drives a time‑multiplexed two‑digit 7‑segment display without visible flicker and with balanced brightness. Use idiomatic SystemVerilog (e.g., logic, always_ff, enumerated states for FSMs). Provide clean module boundaries and keep all state synchronous. Include brief comments explaining the design choices.



AI Response

// Clock divider for iCE40 UP5K internal oscillator (~20 MHz)
// Generates scan clock (~100-200 Hz) and display clock (~500 Hz)

module clk_divider (
  input  logic clk_in,      // ~20 MHz internal oscillator
  output logic clk_scan,    // ~100 Hz for keypad scanning
  output logic clk_display  // ~500 Hz for 7-segment multiplexing
);

  localparam int SCAN_DIV = 20_000_000 / (2 * 100);    // Divider for 100 Hz
  localparam int DISP_DIV = 20_000_000 / (2 * 500);    // Divider for 500 Hz

  logic [$clog2(SCAN_DIV)-1:0] scan_cnt;
  logic [$clog2(DISP_DIV)-1:0] disp_cnt;

  // Scan clock divider (100 Hz)
  always_ff @(posedge clk_in) begin
    if (scan_cnt == SCAN_DIV - 1) begin
      scan_cnt &lt;= '0;
      clk_scan &lt;= ~clk_scan;
    end else begin
      scan_cnt &lt;= scan_cnt + 1'b1;
    end
  end

  // Display clock divider (500 Hz)
  always_ff @(posedge clk_in) begin
    if (disp_cnt == DISP_DIV - 1) begin
      disp_cnt &lt;= '0;
      clk_display &lt;= ~clk_display;
    end else begin
      disp_cnt &lt;= disp_cnt + 1'b1;
    end
  end

endmodule

// 4x4 Keypad scanner with debounce-by-design
// Columns are active-low outputs, rows are active-low inputs
// Scans one column at a time, samples all rows
// Registers key only on press-to-release transition, ignores multi-press

module keypad_scanner (
  input  logic clk,            // Scan clock (~100 Hz)
  input  logic [3:0] row,      // Active-low row inputs
  output logic [3:0] col,      // Active-low column outputs (one at a time)
  output logic [3:0] key_code, // Hex code of detected key (row[2:0] | col_idx)
  output logic key_valid       // Pulse when new key is registered
);

  // FSM states for debounce-by-design
  typedef enum logic [1:0] {
    IDLE  = 2'b00,   // No key pressed, waiting for press
    HOLD  = 2'b01,   // Key pressed and held
    WAIT  = 2'b10    // Key released, ready to register
  } state_t;

  state_t state, next_state;
  logic [2:0] col_idx;         // Current column index (0-3)
  logic any_row_active;        // True if any row is active in current column

  // Decode column index to active-low column vector
  always_comb begin
    col = 4'b1111;  // All inactive by default
    col[col_idx] = 1'b0;  // One column pulled low
  end

  // Detect if any row is active (low) in current scan
  assign any_row_active = ~(row[0] & row[1] & row[2] & row[3]);

  // Key code: {row_index[2:0], col_index[1:0]} forms 5-bit code, truncated to 4-bit
  // In practice: row index (0-3) in bits [2:0], col_index (0-3) in bits [1:0]
  always_comb begin
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 4; i++) begin
      if (!row[i]) begin
        key_code = {i[2:0], col_idx[1:0]};
      end
    end
  end

  // Column scanning and FSM update
  always_ff @(posedge clk) begin
    state &lt;= next_state;

    // Always advance to next column for round-robin scanning
    col_idx &lt;= (col_idx == 3'd3) ? 3'd0 : col_idx + 1'b1;
  end

  // Debounce-by-design FSM logic
  always_comb begin
    next_state = state;
    key_valid = 1'b0;

    case (state)
      IDLE: begin
        // Waiting for key press: transition when any row goes active
        if (any_row_active) begin
          next_state = HOLD;
        end
      end

      HOLD: begin
        // Key is pressed and held: stay in HOLD
        if (!any_row_active) begin
          // Key released: register it and move to WAIT for debounce
          key_valid = 1'b1;
          next_state = WAIT;
        end
      end

      WAIT: begin
        // Brief wait after release to ensure debounce
        // Immediately return to IDLE (next clock cycle will have progressed enough)
        next_state = IDLE;
      end

      default: next_state = IDLE;
    endcase
  end

endmodule

// 7-segment display decoder
// Converts 4-bit hex digit to 7-segment pattern (common cathode: active high)
// Segment mapping: [a, b, c, d, e, f, g]

module seg7_decoder (
  input  logic [3:0] hex,    // Hex digit (0-F)
  output logic [6:0] seg7    // 7-segment pattern {a, b, c, d, e, f, g}
);

  always_comb begin
    case (hex)
      4'h0: seg7 = 7'b1110111;  // 0: a, b, c, d, e, f
      4'h1: seg7 = 7'b0100100;  // 1: b, c
      4'h2: seg7 = 7'b1011101;  // 2: a, b, d, e, g
      4'h3: seg7 = 7'b1101101;  // 3: a, b, c, d, g
      4'h4: seg7 = 7'b0101110;  // 4: b, c, f, g
      4'h5: seg7 = 7'b1101011;  // 5: a, c, d, f, g
      4'h6: seg7 = 7'b1111011;  // 6: a, c, d, e, f, g
      4'h7: seg7 = 7'b0100101;  // 7: a, b, c
      4'h8: seg7 = 7'b1111111;  // 8: a, b, c, d, e, f, g (all segments)
      4'h9: seg7 = 7'b1101111;  // 9: a, b, c, d, f, g
      4'hA: seg7 = 7'b0111111;  // A: b, c, e, f, g
      4'hB: seg7 = 7'b1111010;  // B: c, d, e, f, g
      4'hC: seg7 = 7'b1110001;  // C: a, d, e, f
      4'hD: seg7 = 7'b1110100;  // D: b, c, d, e, g
      4'hE: seg7 = 7'b1111001;  // E: a, d, e, f, g
      4'hF: seg7 = 7'b0111001;  // F: e, f, g (common representation)
    endcase
  end

endmodule

// Dual 7-segment display controller with time-multiplexing
// Displays two hex digits with no visible flicker
// Updates at display clock (~500 Hz), alternating between digits

module seg7_display (
  input  logic clk_display,      // Display clock (~500 Hz)
  input  logic [3:0] digit_old,  // Older/left digit
  input  logic [3:0] digit_new,  // Newer/right digit
  output logic [6:0] seg7,       // 7-segment output {a, b, c, d, e, f, g}
  output logic [1:0] digit_sel   // Digit select (active low): 2'b10 = left, 2'b01 = right
);

  logic digit_toggle;            // Alternates between 0 (left) and 1 (right)
  logic [3:0] current_digit;     // Currently displayed digit
  logic [6:0] seg7_raw;          // Raw 7-segment from decoder

  // Instantiate 7-segment decoder
  seg7_decoder decoder (
    .hex(current_digit),
    .seg7(seg7_raw)
  );

  // Toggle between digits on display clock (creates ~250 Hz refresh per digit)
  always_ff @(posedge clk_display) begin
    digit_toggle &lt;= ~digit_toggle;
  end

  // Select which digit to display and generate digit_sel signal
  always_comb begin
    if (digit_toggle) begin
      current_digit = digit_new;    // Right digit (most recent)
      digit_sel = 2'b01;             // Active-low select for right digit
    end else begin
      current_digit = digit_old;     // Left digit (older)
      digit_sel = 2'b10;             // Active-low select for left digit
    end
  end

  // Segment output (passed through from decoder)
  assign seg7 = seg7_raw;

endmodule

// Top-level keypad and display controller for iCE40 UP5K
// Scans 4x4 keypad matrix and displays last two hex keys on dual 7-segment display
// 
// Ports:
//   clk_osc: ~20 MHz internal oscillator
//   row[3:0]: Active-low row inputs from keypad
//   col[3:0]: Active-low column outputs to keypad (drive as open-drain)
//   seg7[6:0]: 7-segment outputs {a, b, c, d, e, f, g}
//   digit_sel[1:0]: Digit select (active low)

module keypad_display_top (
  input  logic clk_osc,
  input  logic [3:0] row,
  output logic [3:0] col,
  output logic [6:0] seg7,
  output logic [1:0] digit_sel
);

  // Internal clock signals
  logic clk_scan;
  logic clk_display;

  // Keypad scanner signals
  logic [3:0] key_code;
  logic key_valid;

  // Display data signals (4-bit hex per digit)
  logic [3:0] digit_old;   // Previous key
  logic [3:0] digit_new;   // Most recent key

  // ==================== Clock Divider ====================
  clk_divider clk_div (
    .clk_in(clk_osc),
    .clk_scan(clk_scan),
    .clk_display(clk_display)
  );

  // ==================== Keypad Scanner ====================
  keypad_scanner scanner (
    .clk(clk_scan),
    .row(row),
    .col(col),
    .key_code(key_code),
    .key_valid(key_valid)
  );

  // ==================== Key History Register ====================
  // When key_valid pulses, shift new key in and old key out
  always_ff @(posedge clk_scan) begin
    if (key_valid) begin
      digit_old &lt;= digit_new;    // Promote most recent to older
      digit_new &lt;= key_code;     // Register new key
    end
  end

  // ==================== 7-Segment Display ====================
  seg7_display display (
    .clk_display(clk_display),
    .digit_old(digit_old),
    .digit_new(digit_new),
    .seg7(seg7),
    .digit_sel(digit_sel)
  );

endmodule

// Testbench for keypad_display_top
